    choose: [['Question an age-related (assumption / assumed).', 'assumption'], ['Training can demonstrate a willingness (to learn / learned).', 'to learn'], ['Employers should assess concerns (individually / individual).', 'individually'], ['Daisuke offers a limited (opportunity / opportunely).', 'opportunity'], ['Unanswered applications do not (prove / proves) one cause.', 'prove'], ['The proposed work is temporary, not a (guaranteed / guaranteeing) permanent role.', 'guaranteed']],
    choices: [['What is directly reported?', 'Some interviewers questioned his ability to adapt.', 'A tribunal has proved the cause of every rejection.', 'A'], ['What do the courses show in this account?', 'That no further assessment is needed for any role.', 'Actions demonstrating willingness to learn.', 'B'], ['How should the café offer be described?', 'Temporary administrative work with limited promises.', 'Permanent employment already secured for life.', 'A'], ['Which point remains uncertain?', 'That he previously managed office work.', 'How much age assumptions explain the overall application results.', 'B']],
    mission: { situation: 'You are Emi. Your tutor is Mr. Hayashi. He is fifty-eight, lost his job through restructuring and applied for more than thirty positions. He has completed training and reports age-related questions. Daisuke offers temporary administrative work.', task: 'Represent the evidence, question stereotypes and clarify what the offer does and does not settle.', roles: ['Mr. Hayashi', 'Emi'], lines: ['Emi, which facts would you keep in an accurate account of my applications?', 'How could you discuss the technology questions without claiming every rejection has the same cause?', 'What do the training courses add?', 'Which concerns should be assessed individually?', 'How would you describe Daisuke’s offer without overstating it?'], evidence: 'Fifty-eight-year-old former office manager, restructuring job loss, more than thirty applications, often no response, some technology/adaptation questions, courses completed. No proved age discrimination finding, employer identity, exact qualifications, compensation or permanent job. Café offer temporary administrative work; no start date or full recovery confirmed. This Mr. Hayashi is separate from the earlier Level 7 entertainment lawyer Ms. Hayashi. No legal advice.', useful: ['He reports ..., while ... remains uncertain.', 'The questions suggest a concern about ...', 'The courses demonstrate ...', 'An individual assessment would consider ...', 'The offer is limited to ...'] },
    topics: [['ASSUMPTIONS', ['How can a question reflect an assumption about a group?', 'What can an individual’s actions add to the assessment?', 'Why distinguish a concern about bias from a proven cause?']], ['CAREER CHANGES', ['What could experience contribute to a different role?', 'Which expectations might need individual discussion?', 'Why avoid assuming that experience prevents learning?']], ['OPPORTUNITIES', ['How can a limited offer still matter?', 'What should remain clear about temporary work?', 'Why avoid promising an outcome before responsibilities are discussed?']]],
    trivia: '“More than thirty” sets a lower bound without giving an exact total. It should not become exactly thirty in a summary, nor an invented precise figure. The same care applies to interpreting the reasons behind those applications’ outcomes.', triviaQuestion: ['Does more than thirty mean exactly thirty applications?', 'No. The exact larger total is not given.'],
    closing: 'Mr. Hayashi describes unanswered applications and questions about adaptation while showing a willingness to learn. Daisuke offers temporary work. The account questions stereotypes without proving every rejection’s cause or guaranteeing permanent employment.',
